Endometriosis
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Cyclical pelvic pain/dysmenorrhoea, Deep dyspareunia, Uterosacral nodularity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-015
Reason for Visit: Acute, intractable, deep pelvic pain and severe pain with bowel movements causing distress and inability to mobilize.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute exacerbation of severe, deep pelvic pain radiating to the rectum, accompanied by intense dyschezia (painful defecation) leading to a vasovagal episode. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to provide potent parenteral analgesia, rule out acute bowel obstruction or ovarian torsion, and stabilize the patient before the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The pain deep in my pelvis is throbbing constantly, and when I tried to go to the toilet, the shooting pain up my rectum made me pass out.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms escalated sharply 12 hours ago, two days prior to the expected onset of menstruation. The patient reports chronic, deep pelvic pain that consistently peaks just before and during menstruation. Tonight, she experienced severe dyschezia (pain with bowel movements) which triggered a vasovagal pre-syncopal episode. She also reports a chronic history of deep dyspareunia worsening peri-menstrually, and chronic fatigue that exacerbates with these pain flares. She has been trying to conceive for two years (subfertility). Denies hematochezia, melena, or urinary symptoms.
Functional Status: Bedbound, lying in a fetal position, unable to tolerate sitting or walking.
Social/Safety Concerns: High impact on quality of life and relationship distress; risk of fall from vasovagal symptoms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Patient appears exhausted, tearful, and is actively guarding her lower abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals deep suprapubic and bilateral lower quadrant tenderness, right greater than left, with no rebound or rigidity. Bowel sounds are normal. Speculum examination (performed with extreme care) shows a normal cervix. Bimanual pelvic examination is intensely painful for the patient; reveals a fixed, retroverted uterus. Extreme tenderness and distinct nodularity are palpated along the uterosacral ligaments posteriorly. A tender, boggy fullness is noted in the right adnexa (suspected endometrioma).
Environmental Observations: Clean environment, partner is highly supportive but anxious.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol 1g and ibuprofen 400mg every 6 hours with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Endometriosis (Acute pre-menstrual flare with severe dyschezia) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Diverticulitis - Unlikely given the young age, strict cyclical nature of the pain, and absence of fever or elevated inflammatory markers.

Ovarian Torsion - Unlikely as the pain, while severe, is an exacerbation of a chronic, predictable cyclical pattern rather than a sudden, acute, ischemic event, though an endometrioma torsion remains a distinct possibility if pain characteristics change.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Ruled out by the absence of fever, normal vaginal discharge, and the classic nodularity on the uterosacral ligaments.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to syncopal episodes)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, incapacitating exacerbation of cyclical pelvic pain and severe dyschezia, leading to vasovagal instability. Objective examination confirmed classic signs of deep infiltrating endometriosis, including a fixed retroverted uterus and exquisite uterosacral nodularity. The severity of the pain, rendering oral analgesia entirely ineffective and causing functional immobility and presyncope, strictly required urgent after-hours intervention. The administration of potent parenteral analgesics and antiemetics was necessary to break the pain cycle and prevent an otherwise inevitable emergency department presentation for acute pain management prior to the next in-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ketorolac 30mg for potent NSAID relief, and subcutaneous fentanyl 50mcg for acute breakthrough visceral pain. Prescribed oral tramadol 50mg as needed for severe breakthrough pain over the next 3 days. Arranged for a routine GP follow-up to discuss referral for diagnostic laparoscopy.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent constipation and reduce pain during bowel movements, take a daily osmotic laxative (e.g., macrogol) starting three days before your period; experiencing softer, pain-free stools will reinforce proactive bowel management. To manage muscle tension during a flare, practice deep diaphragmatic breathing for 10 minutes when the pain spikes; feeling the pelvic floor muscles relax will encourage the use of mindfulness pain techniques.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in one week to discuss long-term management and specialist gynecological referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the pain suddenly localizes to one side and becomes sharp and tearing, if you develop a fever, or if you become completely unable to pass gas or have a bowel movement for more than 24 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Definitive diagnosis of endometriosis requires direct visualization via laparoscopy; the current assessment is highly probable based on clinical exam.
Clinical Course: The injections will provide profound relief for the next 4 to 6 hours. You must stay ahead of the pain with the prescribed oral medications over the next few days.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if the pain changes to a sudden, sharp, one-sided tearing sensation, or if you begin vomiting continuously.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Endometriosis involves tissue similar to the womb lining growing outside the uterus, such as on the ligaments near your bowel. As your period approaches, this tissue bleeds and causes severe inflammation and pain, especially when you use your bowels. The strong injections provided will rapidly reduce this inflammation and block the acute pain signals.
Actionable Steps: Start taking a gentle laxative immediately to soften your stools. Take the tramadol precisely at the onset of severe pain, do not wait for it to become unbearable.

